// ==============================================================================
// NEXUS — FIRESTORE SECURITY RULES  ·  P R O P O S A L   ·   N O T   W I R E D
// ==============================================================================
//
// ⛔ THIS FILE IS INERT. NOTHING DEPLOYS IT.
//
//    `firebase.json` has NO `firestore` section and this task deliberately did
//    not add one. `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` deploys `functions` and
//    `hosting` only and was not touched. Therefore:
//
//      • `firebase deploy` (no flags) will NOT deploy this file.
//      • The CI pipeline on `main` will NOT deploy this file.
//      • The rules currently live in project `idc-app-e0c59` are WHATEVER IS IN
//        THE FIREBASE CONSOLE. Nobody in this repository knows what they are.
//        This file is a PROPOSAL for what they should become, not a record of
//        what they are.
//
//    Wiring it up is a deliberate human act. See `firestore.rules.README.md`
//    §7 for the two-line `firebase.json` change and why you should read §3
//    (Rules Playground) and §5 (rollback) BEFORE making it.
//
//    Why the caution is not theatre: the master duty roster of four practising
//    clinicians is rewritten CLIENT-SIDE from a colleague's browser. A rule
//    that is one predicate too strict does not degrade the app — it locks
//    Brandon, Ying Xian, Derlinder and Fadzlynn out of their live working week,
//    silently, because two of the three affected listeners still have no error
//    callback (`ROSTER_QC_AUDIT.md` M8 fixed the roster and swap listeners; the
//    feed and pulse listeners are still silent).
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW THIS WAS DERIVED
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Not from a template and not from the collection list in anybody's head. Every
// path below was found by grepping `doc(`, `collection(`, `setDoc`, `addDoc`,
// `updateDoc`, `onSnapshot`, `getDoc`, `getDocs`, `deleteDoc`, `query(` across
// `src/` and `functions/`, then reading each call site to establish who reaches
// it and under what UI condition. `firestore.rules.README.md` §2 carries the
// full call-site table, path by path and line by line.
//
// Three facts from that sweep shape everything here:
//
//   1. CLOUD FUNCTIONS DO NOT PASS THROUGH THESE RULES. `functions/index.js`
//      uses the Admin SDK (`getFirestore()` / `admin.firestore()`), which
//      bypasses security rules entirely. So `resources` (read only by
//      `publicTriageChat`), and `feed_posts` CREATION and EDITING (done by the
//      `processFeedPost` callable), need no client permission at all. Locking
//      them here costs the app nothing. This is the single biggest source of
//      free least-privilege in the whole file.
//
//   2. THE DOCUMENTS ARE KEYED BY HUMAN NAME AND DIRECTORY ID, NOT BY UID.
//      `shift_swaps.targetStaff` is `"Ying Xian"`. `system_data/daily_pulse`
//      keys are `"Brandon"`. `wellbeing_history/{id}` is `"fadzlynn"`.
//      `notifications.recipient` is a name. Only `users/{uid}` is keyed by
//      `request.auth.uid` — and even that collection is ALSO written under the
//      directory id (see the `users` block). Rules therefore cannot authorise
//      on `request.auth.uid` alone; they must map the authenticated email to a
//      display name and a directory id. That map is `DIRECTORY` below.
//
//   3. THE `user?.role === 'admin'` CHECKS IN THE UI ARE NOT A BOUNDARY, AND
//      WORSE, THEY ARE SELF-ASSIGNABLE. `App.jsx:174` merges the
//      `users/{uid}` document OVER the directory profile
//      (`{ ...initialProfile, ...docSnap.data() }`), and `ProfileView.jsx:75`
//      lets any user write a `role` field into their own `users/{uid}`
//      document. Typing "admin" into the profile Role box therefore satisfies
//      `hasAdminAccess` in `App.jsx:314` and `user?.role === 'admin'` in
//      `RosterView.jsx:613`/`:644`. Nothing below trusts that field. `isAdmin()`
//      here is an EMAIL allowlist, matching `App.jsx:313`'s `ADMIN_EMAILS`.
//      (The `users` block also refuses to store `role: 'admin'` from a
//      non-admin, so the escalation stops being available in the UI too — but
//      that is a courtesy, not the boundary.)
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THIS FILE COSTS TO OWN — read before you like it
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `DIRECTORY` below is a THIRD copy of the team. `ROSTER_POSTMORTEM.md` C3
// already indicts the second one (`RosterView.jsx`'s hardcoded pool vs
// `TEAM_DIRECTORY`), and C-RC2 names the root cause: "a constant embedded in a
// component instead of imported". This file cannot import anything — Firestore
// rules have no module system — so the duplication is unavoidable in rules
// alone. It is therefore a KNOWN, ACCEPTED defect of this design, not an
// oversight:
//
//   • Onboarding or offboarding a clinician now requires editing
//     `src/utils/index.js` AND this file AND redeploying rules. Miss the second
//     step and the new joiner is authenticated but cannot read the roster.
//   • The only durable fix is to stop keying documents by display name. That is
//     an application change (decision D4 territory), not a rules change.
//   • `firestore.rules.README.md` §8 carries this as a standing maintenance
//     hazard with the exact edit procedure.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THESE RULES CANNOT DO — stated up front, not buried
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   • They cannot verify a swap. See the `system_data/roster_2026` block: the
//     honest ceiling is "one existing day, one field, by a team member",
//     because the accepting client never tells Firestore WHICH swap it is
//     acting on. Full detail in that block's comment.
//   • They cannot rate-limit, and they cannot see query `where` clauses.
//   • They do not apply to the Firebase console, the Admin SDK, or any Cloud
//     Function. "Write-only" below means write-only TO CLIENTS.
//   • They cannot be unit-tested in this repo's `npm test` (no emulator in CI).
//     The verification path is the Rules Playground cases in the runbook, plus
//     the local emulator run recorded in `firestore.rules.README.md` §4.
//
// rules_version 2 is required: `hasOnly` on lists, `MapDiff.affectedKeys()`,
// `Map.get(key, default)` and recursive wildcards all depend on it.
// ==============================================================================

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 1 — IDENTITY
    // ==========================================================================
    //
    // Transcribed from `TEAM_DIRECTORY` in `src/utils/index.js:22-95`, verbatim,
    // with every key lower-cased because `request.auth.token.email` preserves
    // the case the account was created with and the directory itself is
    // inconsistent about it ('Evelyn.Ong.MH@kkh.com.sg', 'Mini.Abraham@kkh...').
    // `checkAccess()` in that file compares `.toLowerCase()` on both sides; this
    // does the same.
    //
    // TWO ENTRIES ARE DELIBERATELY PRESENT AND CANNOT LOG IN TODAY.
    // `WelcomeScreen.jsx:107` rejects any address not ending `@kkh.com.sg`
    // BEFORE it consults the directory, so `benny.loo.k.g.@singhealth.com.sg`
    // and `mohammad.ashik.zainuddin@singhealth.com.sg` are unreachable through
    // the login form regardless of what these rules say. They are listed anyway
    // so that fixing the login gate does not require a second rules deploy, and
    // so that the discrepancy is visible rather than silently encoded.
    // (`benny.loo.k.g.@` also has a trailing dot in its local part, which is not
    // a valid RFC 5321 address — Firebase Auth may refuse to create it at all.
    // Flagged in the runbook, not fixed here: guessing at a colleague's real
    // address is exactly the kind of unverified claim this repo does not do.)
    function directory() {
      return {
        // --- LEADERSHIP & ADMINS ---
        'muhammad.alif@kkh.com.sg':              { 'name': 'Alif',      'id': 'alif' },
        'siti.nur.anisah.nh@kkh.com.sg':         { 'name': 'Nisa',      'id': 'nisa' },
        'evelyn.ong.mh@kkh.com.sg':              { 'name': 'Evelyn',    'id': 'evelyn' },
        // --- MEDICAL & NURSING LEADS (VIEWERS) ---
        'benny.loo.k.g.@singhealth.com.sg':      { 'name': 'Benny',     'id': 'benny' },
        'mohammad.ashik.zainuddin@singhealth.com.sg': { 'name': 'Ashik', 'id': 'ashik' },
        'mini.abraham@kkh.com.sg':               { 'name': 'Mini',      'id': 'mini' },
        // --- CLINICAL EXERCISE PHYSIOLOGISTS (STAFF) ---
        'brandon.feng.gg@kkh.com.sg':            { 'name': 'Brandon',   'id': 'brandon' },
        'lim.ying.xian@kkh.com.sg':              { 'name': 'Ying Xian', 'id': 'ying_xian' },
        'derlinder.kaur@kkh.com.sg':             { 'name': 'Derlinder', 'id': 'derlinder' },
        'fadzlynn.mohamad.fadzully@kkh.com.sg':  { 'name': 'Fadzlynn',  'id': 'fadzlynn' }
      };
    }

    // The display names, as a list, for validating that a swap actually names a
    // colleague. Must stay in step with `directory()` above — there is no way to
    // derive one from the other in this language (`Map.values()` does not exist).
    function directoryNames() {
      return ['Alif', 'Nisa', 'Evelyn', 'Benny', 'Ashik', 'Mini',
              'Brandon', 'Ying Xian', 'Derlinder', 'Fadzlynn'];
    }

    // `role: 'admin'` in `TEAM_DIRECTORY`, which is also exactly
    // `ADMIN_EMAILS` at `App.jsx:313`. Two sources, same two people, checked.
    function adminEmails() {
      return ['muhammad.alif@kkh.com.sg', 'siti.nur.anisah.nh@kkh.com.sg'];
    }

    // Lower-cased email of the caller, or '' when there is no usable identity.
    // Guarded three ways because `request.auth.token.email` is absent for some
    // providers and null-dereferencing in rules is an evaluation ERROR, which
    // Firestore treats as DENY — a silent lockout rather than a clean false.
    function callerEmail() {
      return request.auth != null
             && request.auth.token != null
             && request.auth.token.email != null
             ? request.auth.token.email.lower()
             : '';
    }

    function isSignedIn() {
      return request.auth != null && request.auth.uid != null;
    }

    // The single gate on essentially everything: signed in AND on the allowlist.
    // A Firebase Auth account that is not in the directory (someone who created
    // one straight against the public API key, bypassing `WelcomeScreen`) gets
    // nothing at all.
    function isMember() {
      return isSignedIn() && callerEmail() in directory();
    }

    function isAdmin() {
      return isSignedIn() && callerEmail() in adminEmails();
    }

    // The caller's roster display name / Firestore directory id.
    // Only ever called behind `isMember()`; the `in` guard prevents the map
    // lookup from erroring on an unknown key.
    function myName() {
      return directory()[callerEmail()].name;
    }

    function myId() {
      return directory()[callerEmail()].id;
    }

    // DELIBERATELY NOT REQUIRED: `request.auth.token.email_verified`.
    //
    // `WelcomeScreen.jsx:114-118` already refuses to complete a login for an
    // unverified account, so this would be belt-and-braces. It is left OFF
    // because an ID token is cached for up to an hour: a clinician who verifies
    // their address mid-session carries `email_verified: false` in their token
    // until it refreshes, and would be denied everything for that hour with no
    // explanation. Turning it on is a one-line change to `isMember()` and
    // `isAdmin()` — see `firestore.rules.README.md` §8, "Optional hardening".
    // FLAGGED FOR YOUR DECISION.

    // ── Shared shape helpers ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // A roster date key, e.g. "2026-02-02". Both engines emit exactly this via
    // local getters (`auraEngine.js:69 toDateKey`,
    // `rosterEngineV2.js:2193 toLocalDateKey`) — post-mortem Block B is the
    // reason the format is worth pinning rather than trusting.
    function isDateKey(k) {
      return k.matches('^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$');
    }

    // True when this write changes nothing outside the keys the document
    // already had. Used to stop a non-admin INVENTING a top-level key on a
    // shared document while still letting them modify an existing one.
    function addsNoNewKeys() {
      return request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(resource.data.keys());
    }

    function changedKeys() {
      return request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys();
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 2 — THE MASTER DUTY ROSTER
    // ==========================================================================
    //
    // Readers  : every directory member (`RosterView.jsx:371` onSnapshot).
    // Writers  : (a) whoever presses Generate — `RosterView.jsx:575`
    //                `setDoc(..., { merge: true })`, MANY date keys at once;
    //            (b) a colleague accepting a swap — `AuraPulseBot.jsx:484`
    //                `updateDoc(rosterRef, { [plan.dateKey]: plan.shifts })`,
    //                EXACTLY ONE date key.
    //
    // That asymmetry is the whole design of this block, and it is the one piece
    // of real leverage rules have here. (a) is the destructive operation — C2 in
    // the post-mortem, "generation overwrites everything" — and it is restricted
    // to the two admins. (b) is a one-day edit and is open to any team member.
    //
    // ⚠️ BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, THE BIGGEST IN THIS FILE. Today the Configure →
    //    Generate Roster button at `RosterView.jsx:813` is NOT admin-gated in
    //    the UI at all: any of the ten directory members can press it and
    //    rewrite the roster. Under these rules only Alif and Nisa can. If a CEP
    //    presses Generate they get a clean, honest failure — `RosterView.jsx:592`
    //    already renders `permission-denied` with "The roster was NOT saved" and
    //    keeps their configuration — but they DID have that power yesterday and
    //    will not tomorrow. Confirm this is what you want before deploying.
    //    (`ROSTER_TODO.md` D5 asks a related question and is still open.)
    //
    // ── HOW NARROWLY COULD (b) BE SCOPED? THE HONEST ANSWER ──────────────────
    //
    // What you would WANT to require is: "this write is permitted because there
    // exists an APPROVED swap naming this caller as `targetStaff` for this
    // date". Rules cannot express that, for three independent reasons, and no
    // amount of cleverness removes any of them:
    //
    //   1. THE SWAP IS STILL PENDING AT WRITE TIME. `AuraPulseBot.jsx` was
    //      deliberately reordered for QC finding M9 so the ledger is flipped to
    //      APPROVED only AFTER a verified roster read-back (`:506`, after
    //      `:484`). So at the instant of the roster write there is no APPROVED
    //      document to point at. Requiring one would deadlock the feature.
    //      Reversing the order to satisfy rules would REINTRODUCE M9 — an
    //      APPROVED ledger entry against an untouched roster. Not a trade worth
    //      making.
    //   2. RULES CANNOT QUERY. `get()` needs a full document path. The swap's
    //      id comes from `addDoc` (`RosterView.jsx:709`), so it is a random
    //      auto-id, and the accepting client does not send it with the roster
    //      write. There is nothing to `get()`.
    //   3. RULES CANNOT INDEX INTO A DIFF. `changedKeys()` is a Set with no
    //      element accessor, so this block can prove that exactly ONE key
    //      changed but cannot name it, cannot regex it, and cannot inspect the
    //      shift array that was written into it. Field-level enforcement of a
    //      swap — "you may only replace yourself into the coLead slot of the
    //      AM Clinic (Ortho) duty" — is therefore NOT ACHIEVABLE IN RULES ALONE.
    //      Anyone who tells you otherwise has not tried it.
    //
    // The ceiling actually reached below, stated as a capability so you can
    // judge it: a signed-in team member may replace the entire shift array of
    // ONE day that ALREADY EXISTS in the document. They cannot create the
    // document, cannot delete it, cannot add a day, cannot remove a day, cannot
    // touch two days in one write, and cannot blank the roster. The residual
    // risk is that a team member can write a wrong-but-well-formed single day —
    // i.e. rules reduce this from "any member can destroy the roster" to "any
    // member can vandalise one day, attributably, and the other nine days are
    // untouched".
    //
    // THE ONLY REAL FIX is to stop writing the roster from a browser: a
    // `applySwap({ swapId })` callable Cloud Function reads the swap, does the
    // substitution with the Admin SDK, and this block becomes
    // `allow write: if isAdmin()`. That is an application change and is out of
    // scope for a rules proposal. Recorded in the runbook as the recommended
    // next step, and it is what would finally close C4/D6 properly.
    match /system_data/roster_2026 {
      allow get: if isMember();
      allow list: if false;              // nothing lists `system_data`.

      // (a) Generation. Admins only. `setDoc(..., { merge: true })` is a create
      //     when the document is absent and an update when it is present, so
      //     both verbs are needed.
      allow create, update: if isAdmin();

      // (b) One day, in place, by a team member completing a swap.
      //
      //     A `resource != null` guard was here in the first draft, to stop this
      //     path being used to CREATE the roster. MUTATION-CHECKING PROVED IT
      //     COULD NOT FAIL and it was removed: Firestore only evaluates an
      //     `update` rule when the document already exists, so `resource` is
      //     never null here and the predicate was unreachable dead weight
      //     dressed as a safety check. The protection it claimed to give comes
      //     from the verb split instead — `create` above is granted only to
      //     `isAdmin()`, so a member cannot bring this document into existence.
      //     Measured: README §4 case R11. This repo does not keep checks that
      //     cannot fail (`ROSTER_POSTMORTEM.md` A-RC4's sibling problem).
      allow update: if isMember()
                    && changedKeys().size() == 1
                    && addsNoNewKeys();

      // Nothing in the app deletes the roster document, so nothing may.
      allow delete: if false;
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 3 — SHIFT SWAP REQUESTS
    // ==========================================================================
    //
    // Created  : `RosterView.jsx:709` addDoc.
    // Read     : `AuraPulseBot.jsx:130-133`, a LIST query filtered
    //            `where('targetStaff','==',myName)` + `where('status','==','PENDING')`.
    // Updated  : `AuraPulseBot.jsx:455` `{status:'DENIED'}` and `:506`
    //            `{status:'APPROVED', approvedAt}`.
    // Deleted  : never, anywhere.
    //
    // Today this is an UNGUARDED `addDoc` from any signed-in client — C4's
    // headline. Anyone could forge a request naming any two colleagues, and
    // anyone could flip anyone else's request to APPROVED.
    //
    // The read rule is a disjunction whose FIRST clause is exactly the app's
    // query predicate, which is what makes the listener keep working: Firestore
    // evaluates a list rule against every document the query would return, and
    // every document that query returns satisfies `targetStaff == myName()`.
    // The `requestedBy` / `initiatedBy` clauses widen it to the people the
    // request is ABOUT — no code reads by those fields yet, but decision D3
    // ("should the requester be told the outcome?") is open, and pre-authorising
    // the read costs nothing while forgetting to would make D3 look like a rules
    // bug later.
    match /shift_swaps/{swapId} {
      allow read: if isMember()
                  && (
                       resource.data.targetStaff == myName()
                       || resource.data.requestedBy == myName()
                       || resource.data.get('initiatedBy', '') == myName()
                       || isAdmin()
                     );

      // ── CREATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      // The exact field set written by `RosterView.jsx:709-731`. `initiatedBy`
      // is the only optional one — `submitSwapRequest` spreads it in ONLY for an
      // on-behalf request, and that absence is load-bearing: it is how a
      // self-request stays byte-identical to the pre-M11 document shape.
      //
      // Two legitimate authors, straight out of `resolveSwapSubject`
      // (`auraEngine.js:579-658`):
      //
      //   SELF     — `requestedBy` is the caller, no `initiatedBy` at all.
      //   ON BEHALF— an ADMIN arranging cover: `requestedBy` is the clinician who
      //              holds the duty, `initiatedBy` is the admin. Restricting this
      //              to admins matches `canArrangeForOthers`
      //              (`RosterView.jsx:644`) and, critically, means a non-admin
      //              cannot mint a request in a colleague's name.
      //
      // `status == 'PENDING'` on create is what makes the update rule below
      // meaningful — without it a forger could create an already-APPROVED
      // document and skip the transition check entirely.
      allow create: if isMember()
                    && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly([
                         'requestedBy', 'targetStaff', 'originalShiftDate',
                         'originalTask', 'swapRole', 'initiatedBy',
                         'reason', 'status', 'timestamp'
                       ])
                    && request.resource.data.keys().hasAll([
                         'requestedBy', 'targetStaff', 'originalShiftDate',
                         'originalTask', 'swapRole', 'status', 'timestamp'
                       ])
                    && request.resource.data.status == 'PENDING'
                    // Both parties must be real colleagues, and a swap with
                    // yourself is not a swap.
                    && request.resource.data.targetStaff in directoryNames()
                    && request.resource.data.requestedBy in directoryNames()
                    && request.resource.data.targetStaff != request.resource.data.requestedBy
                    // A3: the duty being handed over. `resolveSwapSubject` now
                    // refuses to produce anything else, and M11 made a null here
                    // unwritable; this is the server-side latch for the same
                    // invariant.
                    && request.resource.data.swapRole in ['lead', 'coLead']
                    && isDateKey(request.resource.data.originalShiftDate)
                    && request.resource.data.originalTask is string
                    && request.resource.data.originalTask.size() > 0
                    && request.resource.data.originalTask.size() <= 200
                    && request.resource.data.reason is string
                    && request.resource.data.reason.size() <= 2000
                    // `serverTimestamp()` resolves to `request.time` during
                    // evaluation, so this pins the ledger clock to the server and
                    // makes a back-dated request unwritable.
                    && request.resource.data.timestamp == request.time
                    && (
                         // SELF
                         (
                           request.resource.data.requestedBy == myName()
                           && !('initiatedBy' in request.resource.data)
                         )
                         ||
                         // ON BEHALF (admins only)
                         (
                           isAdmin()
                           && request.resource.data.get('initiatedBy', '') == myName()
                         )
                       );

      // ── UPDATE: the PENDING → APPROVED / DENIED transition ────────────────
      // Only the named `targetStaff` — the person actually being asked to cover —
      // and only out of PENDING, so an outcome cannot be re-litigated or
      // reversed by anyone once recorded. Every identifying field is pinned
      // unchanged by `hasOnly`: the ledger entry that gets approved must be the
      // one that was created.
      //
      // `approvedAt` is written by `AuraPulseBot.jsx:508` as
      // `new Date().toISOString()` — a CLIENT clock, and a string, not a
      // timestamp. It is accepted as-is here because changing it is an app
      // change; it is not trusted for anything. (`request.time` would be the
      // right value. Noted in the runbook's follow-ups.)
      allow update: if isMember()
                    && resource.data.status == 'PENDING'
                    && resource.data.targetStaff == myName()
                    && request.resource.data.status in ['APPROVED', 'DENIED']
                    && changedKeys().hasOnly(['status', 'approvedAt']);

      // The app never deletes a swap, and the ledger is the audit trail for a
      // change to somebody's working week. Nothing may delete it.
      allow delete: if false;
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 4 — WELLBEING
    // ==========================================================================

    // ── Per-clinician wellbeing history ──────────────────────────────────────
    // Written : `AuraPulseBot.jsx:555`
    //           `setDoc(wellbeing_history/{user.id}, { logs: arrayUnion(...) }, { merge: true })`.
    //           `user.id` is the TEAM_DIRECTORY id ('fadzlynn'), NOT the auth uid
    //           — see the `users` block for why that distinction matters.
    // Read    : `AdminWellbeingPanel.jsx:24` `getDocs(collection(...))`, a LIST
    //           of the whole collection, rendered as a 7-day burnout monitor
    //           captioned "CONFIDENTIAL: For HOD/Lead use only".
    //
    // This is the most sensitive collection in the project: a longitudinal
    // energy/burnout record per named clinician. Owner + admins, nobody else.
    //
    // `get` AND `list` ARE SPLIT DELIBERATELY, and the split was forced by a
    // measurement, not by taste. `read` covers both verbs, and with a single
    // `allow read` here the `isAdmin()` disjunct silently re-opened
    // `_anonymous_logs` to admins — a `false` in the literal block below cannot
    // subtract from an `allow` in this one, because rules are OR'd across
    // matching blocks and there is no `deny`. The emulator run caught it
    // (`firestore.rules.README.md` §4, case W10). Splitting the verbs fixes it:
    //   • `get`  — per document, and explicitly excludes the anonymous bucket.
    //   • `list` — admins only; list requests never consult `get` rules, which
    //              is exactly what keeps `AdminWellbeingPanel`'s unfiltered
    //              `getDocs` working while a targeted read of the bucket stays
    //              refused.
    //
    // No update-shape restriction beyond ownership: the write is an
    // `arrayUnion` append via `setDoc(merge)` and pinning its shape would buy
    // little while risking the one write path a clinician has to their own
    // record.
    match /wellbeing_history/{staffId} {
      allow get: if isMember()
                 && staffId != '_anonymous_logs'
                 && (staffId == myId() || isAdmin());
      allow list: if isAdmin();
      allow create, update: if isMember() && staffId == myId();
      allow delete: if false;           // a wellbeing record is not erasable by
                                        // the app. Console/Admin SDK only.
    }

    // ── The anonymous pulse bucket: WRITE-ONLY ───────────────────────────────
    // Written : `AuraPulseBot.jsx:550-552`, on the `isDemo || persona==='anon'`
    //           branch — `setDoc(merge)` then `updateDoc(arrayUnion(logs))`.
    // Read    : nothing, anywhere, ever. Grepped.
    //
    // So it is write-only against a TARGETED read, by anyone, including admins —
    // that much is measured (README §4, cases W9/W10). It is not write-only
    // against a COLLECTION SCAN, and here is the honest accounting:
    //
    //   • `AdminWellbeingPanel.jsx:24` does `getDocs(collection('wellbeing_history'))`
    //     with no filter. A `list` request is matched by the WILDCARD block
    //     above — a literal-segment match only ever governs a direct document
    //     read — so an admin's collection scan STILL RETURNS this document.
    //     That is not fixable in rules: making the bucket unreadable to a scan
    //     means the scan fails, and the burnout panel silently renders empty
    //     (its `catch` at `:29` only logs). Breaking a clinical-safety panel to
    //     tighten a bucket the panel does not display is the wrong trade.
    //     THE FIX IS A ONE-LINE APP CHANGE: move the bucket out of this
    //     collection — `anonymous_pulse/logs` — after which a genuinely
    //     write-only block can cover it. No rules for that path are written
    //     here, because writing rules for a collection that does not exist is
    //     how a rules file starts lying. The block to add is in the runbook.
    //     FLAGGED FOR YOUR DECISION.
    //   • No rule can hide anything from the Firebase console or the Admin SDK.
    //     "Anonymous" here means unattributed IN THE DATA, and the data does
    //     honour that: `logData` carries no identity, only energy/phase/action.
    //
    // `read: if false` below is therefore DOCUMENTATION of intent, not the
    // mechanism: the mechanism is the `staffId != '_anonymous_logs'` exclusion
    // in the wildcard's `get`. Stated plainly because the first draft of this
    // file got that exact point wrong and the emulator, not the author, found it.
    //
    // ⚠️ BEHAVIOUR CHANGE. `isMember()` means the SANDBOX can no longer write
    //    here. Demo mode is reachable with NO LOGIN AT ALL
    //    (`App.jsx:623` renders the whole app when `isDemo` is true and `user`
    //    is null), so today an anonymous visitor's simulated pulse log lands in
    //    the live collection. Denying that is a bug fix wearing a lockout's
    //    clothing — but it IS a change, and `confirmLog`'s catch will surface
    //    "Could not sync your pulse log" to a sandbox visitor. See the runbook.
    match /wellbeing_history/_anonymous_logs {
      allow read: if false;
      allow create, update: if isMember();
      allow delete: if false;
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 5 — SHARED OPERATIONAL DOCUMENTS IN `system_data`
    // ==========================================================================

    // ── The team pulse heat-map ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Read    : `WellbeingView.jsx:42` and it is the whole point of the view —
    //           every member sees every member's energy. Not a leak; a feature.
    // Written : `WellbeingView.jsx:146` `{ [selectedStaff]: payload }`,
    //           `AuraPulseBot.jsx:553` `{ [heatKey]: payload }` (anon/demo),
    //           `:556` `{ [user.name]: payload }`.
    //
    // The document is a map keyed by display name, so "you may only update your
    // own entry" is expressible exactly, and this is the one place in the file
    // where a diff-based rule lands cleanly. It matches the UI's own
    // `canEdit` at `WellbeingView.jsx:309` (own row, or admin — the extra
    // `user?.name === 'Nisa'` clause there is redundant, Nisa is an admin).
    //
    // ⚠️ BEHAVIOUR CHANGE. The anon/demo branch at `AuraPulseBot.jsx:553`
    //    writes a key like `Anon_4718` (and, in demo, a Marvel persona name).
    //    Neither is the caller's directory name, so both are now DENIED. Same
    //    judgement as the anonymous bucket above: the sandbox should never have
    //    been writing to the live pulse map. Listed in the runbook as a break.
    match /system_data/daily_pulse {
      allow get: if isMember();
      allow list: if false;
      allow create: if isMember()
                    && (isAdmin() || request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly([myName()]));
      allow update: if isMember()
                    && (isAdmin() || changedKeys().hasOnly([myName()]));
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ── Monthly attendance ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Read `App.jsx:274` (all members, feeds the dashboard chart); written only
    // by `AdminPanel.jsx:235`, which lives behind `hasAdminAccess`. Team-level
    // operational counts — admin-write is both least privilege and current
    // intent.
    match /system_data/monthly_attendance {
      allow get: if isMember();
      allow list: if false;
      allow create, update: if isAdmin();
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ── Published annual reports, and the catch-all for `system_data` ────────
    //
    // `system_data/reports_{year}`: read by `SmartReportView.jsx:148` for EVERY
    // user; written by `SmartAnalysis.jsx:121`.
    //
    // ⚠️ KNOWN CONFIDENTIALITY GAP, LEFT OPEN ON PURPOSE — FLAGGED FOR REVIEW.
    //    That document holds BOTH `publicText` and `privateText`. The private
    //    half is the HOD-only analysis, and the UI hides it behind
    //    `isActuallyAdmin` (`SmartReportView.jsx:13`) — which is
    //    `forceAdminView === true`, a prop `App.jsx` never passes, so the
    //    private text is currently fetched into every user's browser memory and
    //    shown to nobody. Restricting READ to admins here would be correct
    //    least privilege AND would blank the public report card for the six
    //    non-admins, because one `getDoc` fetches both fields.
    //
    //    So: read stays open to members, and the gap is named instead of
    //    quietly encoded. THE FIX IS AN APP CHANGE — split into
    //    `reports_{year}` (public) and `reports_{year}_private` (admin), then
    //    tighten this rule. If you would rather take the breakage now, the
    //    stricter form is one word: change `allow get` to `if isAdmin()`.
    //    Recorded in the runbook's decision list; do not let it sit forever.
    //
    // The `{docId}` wildcard is regex-gated so it cannot widen the three
    // documents matched literally above. Anything else that ever appears in
    // `system_data` gets nothing until somebody writes a rule for it — which is
    // the desired default for a collection this load-bearing.
    match /system_data/{docId} {
      allow get: if isMember() && docId.matches('^reports_[0-9]{4}$');
      allow list: if false;
      allow create, update: if isAdmin() && docId.matches('^reports_[0-9]{4}$');
      allow delete: if false;
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 6 — TEAM / PROJECT / WORKLOAD DATA
    // ==========================================================================

    // ── Project portfolio, current year ──────────────────────────────────────
    // Listed by `App.jsx:255` for every member; mutated only by `AdminPanel.jsx`
    // (`:262`, `:287`, `:306`, `:327`), all behind `hasAdminAccess`.
    // `AdminPanel.jsx:327` uses a `writeBatch`; batched writes are evaluated per
    // document against the same rules, so nothing special is needed.
    match /cep_team/{staffId} {
      allow get, list: if isMember();
      allow create, update: if isAdmin();
      allow delete: if false;           // no delete path exists in the app.
    }

    // ── Historical archives, one collection per year ─────────────────────────
    // `archive_2025`, `archive_2024`, … Listed by `App.jsx:255` (year selector)
    // and `AdminPanel.jsx:86`; written by `AdminPanel.jsx:181/:184` and
    // `SmartAnalysis.jsx:133`.
    //
    // A single-segment collection wildcard is used rather than enumerating
    // years, because enumerating them means this file silently expires on
    // 1 Jan — a maintenance trap worse than the wildcard. The regex is what
    // keeps it from being a hole: this block grants NOTHING to any collection
    // whose name is not literally `archive_` + four digits, and it is the only
    // collection-wildcard block in the file for exactly that reason.
    match /{archiveCollection}/{staffId} {
      allow get, list: if isMember()
                       && archiveCollection.matches('^archive_[0-9]{4}$');
      allow create, update: if isAdmin()
                            && archiveCollection.matches('^archive_[0-9]{4}$');
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ── Clinical load, 12 numbers per clinician ──────────────────────────────
    // Read : `App.jsx:265` per-document listeners (all members),
    //        `AdminPanel.jsx:110` list.
    // Write: `AdminPanel.jsx:205` (admin UI), and `AuraPulseBot.jsx:852`
    //        — AURA's DATA_ENTRY mode, "Log 35 patients for January", available
    //        to ANY signed-in user with `target_doc` chosen by the language
    //        model from a System Note carrying the caller's own id
    //        (`functions/index.js:241`).
    //
    // `staffId == myId()` preserves that self-service flow exactly while
    // removing the ability to log numbers into a COLLEAGUE's record — which is
    // currently possible, and would be attributed to them. Note the document is
    // keyed by directory id ('ying_xian'); `AdminPanel` derives the same string
    // from the display name, so the two agree.
    //
    // ⚠️ `StaffLoadEditor.jsx` writes `staff_loads/{'Ying Xian'}` — the display
    //    name WITH a space, which nothing reads. It is dead code: no component
    //    imports or renders it. These rules deny those writes. That is a
    //    latent-bug reveal, not a regression, but it belongs on the list.
    match /staff_loads/{staffId} {
      allow get, list: if isMember();
      allow create, update: if isMember() && (isAdmin() || staffId == myId());
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // ── Team-wide workload counters written by AURA's DATA_ENTRY mode ────────
    // `monthly_workload/{mmm_yyyy}` — `functions/index.js:234-236` instructs the
    // model to target it; `AuraPulseBot.jsx:861` performs the write. NOTHING in
    // the app reads it back. These are team-level metrics, so admin-write.
    //
    // ⚠️ BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a non-admin asking AURA to "log 145 attendances for
    //    January" will now get the DATA_ENTRY error bubble instead of a silent
    //    success. Listed in the runbook.
    match /monthly_workload/{period} {
      allow read: if false;             // nothing reads it; nothing may.
      allow create, update: if isAdmin();
      allow delete: if false;
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 7 — USER PROFILES
    // ==========================================================================
    //
    // ⚠️ THIS COLLECTION IS KEYED TWO DIFFERENT WAYS AND BOTH ARE LIVE.
    //
    //    BY AUTH UID   — `App.jsx:174`/`:219` (onSnapshot),
    //                    `NexusContext.jsx:24` (getDoc),
    //                    `ProfileView.jsx:17`/`:60`/`:75`/`:113`,
    //                    `WellbeingView.jsx:52`/`:100` (fcmToken).
    //    BY DIRECTORY ID — `AuraPulseBot.jsx:557`
    //                    `setDoc(users/{user.id}, { aura_last_phase, aura_memory,
    //                    last_interaction }, { merge: true })`, where `user.id`
    //                    is 'alif', not the uid.
    //
    //    So every pulse log writes AURA's memory into `users/alif` while the
    //    entire rest of the app reads `users/<uid>`. That memory is written and
    //    never read back — grep `aura_memory`: one write, no reads. It is a
    //    pre-existing split-brain of exactly the A-RC1 species the post-mortem
    //    is about (one name, two meanings), and it is NOT this proposal's job to
    //    fix it. Both keyings are permitted below so that deploying rules does
    //    not break `confirmLog`. FLAGGED: pick one keying, in the app.
    //
    // `list` is denied — nothing client-side enumerates users. The scheduled
    // nudge does (`functions/index.js:482`) and is Admin SDK, so unaffected.
    //
    // The `role` clause: `ProfileView.jsx:75` legitimately writes a `role`
    // string from the profile form, and `App.jsx:174` merges it OVER the
    // directory profile, so writing `role: 'admin'` grants every
    // `user?.role === 'admin'` affordance in the UI. Nothing in this file trusts
    // that field, so the escalation buys no DATA access — but it does buy a
    // confusing AdminPanel that then fails on every write, so the string is
    // refused for non-admins. Admins may write it because `startEditing`
    // pre-fills the form with their own directory role — literally the string
    // 'admin' — and saving their profile must not fail.
    match /users/{userId} {
      allow get: if isMember() && (userId == request.auth.uid || userId == myId());
      allow list: if false;
      allow create, update: if isMember()
                            && (userId == request.auth.uid || userId == myId())
                            && (isAdmin() || request.resource.data.get('role', '') != 'admin');
      allow delete: if false;
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 8 — FEEDS
    // ==========================================================================
    //
    // Posts are CREATED AND EDITED BY A CLOUD FUNCTION
    // (`functions/index.js:615`/`:625`, `processFeedPost`, Admin SDK), so the
    // client needs no create permission at all and gets none. What the client
    // genuinely does:
    //
    //   read   `FeedsView.jsx:114` list, ordered by timestamp
    //   update `:60`  `{ comments: increment(1) }`
    //          `:204` `{ likes: increment(1) }`
    //   delete `:174` author-gated in the UI at `:268-299`
    //
    // ⚠️ BEHAVIOUR CHANGE. That listener at `:114` has NO `isDemo` guard and NO
    //    error callback, and demo mode needs no login — so today an
    //    unauthenticated sandbox visitor reads the live feed. Under
    //    `isMember()` they will not, and because there is no error callback
    //    they will see only the mock posts with NO indication anything failed.
    //    That silence is the worst part of this change; the app fix is an error
    //    callback (QC M8's sibling, still open for this listener).
    //
    // The counter rule cannot stop a member from liking a post ten times — that
    // is inherent to a client-side `increment()` and would need a
    // `likes/{uid}` subcollection or a Cloud Function to fix. It does stop a
    // member rewriting a colleague's post text through the counter path, which
    // is what an unrestricted update would allow.
    //
    // Delete matches the UI's own author test. Note `FeedsView.jsx:271` treats
    // `post.author === 'Staff Member'` as "you are the author" FOR EVERYONE, so
    // for legacy posts saved under that placeholder the UI will offer a delete
    // button that these rules refuse. Visible failure over silent
    // cross-authorship: correct, but it will look like a bug. On the list.
    match /feed_posts/{postId} {
      allow get, list: if isMember();
      allow create: if false;           // Cloud Function only (Admin SDK).
      allow update: if isMember()
                    && changedKeys().hasOnly(['likes', 'comments']);
      allow delete: if isMember()
                    && (resource.data.author == myName() || isAdmin());

      // Comments: read by `FeedsView.jsx:41`, created at `:59` with exactly
      // `{ author, text, timestamp }`.
      //
      // ⚠️ `author` is `user?.name || 'Staff Member'`, and `user.name` can be
      //    OVERWRITTEN by the profile form (see Section 7). A member who renamed
      //    themselves will be denied here. That same rename ALREADY breaks their
      //    swap listener and their pulse row today, silently — see the runbook's
      //    pre-flight check, which is the single most important thing to do
      //    before deploying this file.
      match /comments/{commentId} {
        allow get, list: if isMember();
        allow create: if isMember()
                      && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['author', 'text', 'timestamp'])
                      && request.resource.data.author == myName()
                      && request.resource.data.text is string
                      && request.resource.data.text.size() > 0
                      && request.resource.data.text.size() <= 5000
                      && request.resource.data.timestamp == request.time;
        allow update: if false;         // no edit path exists in the app.
        allow delete: if false;         // no delete path exists in the app.
      }
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 9 — NOTIFICATIONS
    // ==========================================================================
    //
    // Read   : `App.jsx:225-230`, `where('recipient','==',user.name)`
    //          + `orderBy('timestamp','desc')`.
    // Written: `App.jsx:322` `{ read: true }`.
    // Created: BY NOTHING. Grep `notifications` across `src/` and `functions/`:
    //          one query, one flag update, no writer. The bell is fed from
    //          documents that must be created by hand in the console, or by
    //          something that does not exist yet. `create` is therefore denied —
    //          if a Cloud Function is built for it (decision D3, "tell the
    //          requester the outcome"), it will use the Admin SDK and bypass
    //          this rule anyway.
    //
    // Same list-rule mechanics as `shift_swaps`: the read predicate is the app's
    // query predicate, so the query is satisfiable.
    match /notifications/{notificationId} {
      allow read: if isMember() && resource.data.recipient == myName();
      allow create: if false;
      allow update: if isMember()
                    && resource.data.recipient == myName()
                    && changedKeys().hasOnly(['read']);
      allow delete: if false;
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 10 — WRITE-ONLY SINKS
    // ==========================================================================

    // ── Beta feedback ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // `FeedbackWidget.jsx:29` addDoc; never read by any client. Create-only,
    // shape-pinned, size-capped.
    //
    // ⚠️ BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: the widget renders in the unauthenticated sandbox
    //    (`App.jsx:633`), so an anonymous visitor can file feedback today and
    //    will get "Could not send feedback" tomorrow. A deliberate consequence
    //    of "authentication required everywhere". If you want sandbox feedback
    //    back, the honest way is a Cloud Function with App Check — not an open
    //    create rule on a collection anyone can discover.
    match /beta_feedback/{feedbackId} {
      allow read: if false;
      allow create: if isMember()
                    && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly([
                         'type', 'message', 'reportedBy', 'environment',
                         'timestamp', 'userAgent'
                       ])
                    && request.resource.data.message is string
                    && request.resource.data.message.size() > 0
                    && request.resource.data.message.size() <= 10000
                    && request.resource.data.timestamp == request.time;
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // ── AURA's document sink ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // `AuraPulseBot.jsx:753` and `:792` `setDoc(smart_database/{docId})` with
    // `docId` = `audit_${Date.now()}` / `doc_${Date.now()}`. Never read back.
    //
    // Create-only means a `Date.now()` COLLISION (two documents minted in the
    // same millisecond) is denied rather than silently overwriting the earlier
    // one. For an "audit" sink that is the behaviour you want, and it is why
    // `update` is not granted here.
    //
    // Note the sandbox writes here too, with `isDemo: true` in the payload, so
    // demo content is mixed into the same collection as live content. Not a
    // rules problem — a data-hygiene one. Noted for the record.
    match /smart_database/{docId} {
      allow read: if false;
      allow create: if isMember()
                    && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly([
                         'timestamp', 'displayDate', 'author', 'role',
                         'content', 'type', 'isDemo', 'silentlyLogged'
                       ])
                    && request.resource.data.author == myName()
                    && request.resource.data.content is string
                    && request.resource.data.content.size() <= 100000;
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // ── Community screening telemetry ────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // ⚠️⚠️ THE ONE DECISION THIS FILE CANNOT MAKE FOR YOU. READ THIS BLOCK.
    //
    // `src/utils/telemetry.js:13` addDoc's into `community_assessments` from
    // `ConventionalForm.jsx:676`, `AuraChat.jsx:814` and `ResultPage.jsx:529/568/577`.
    // All five call sites live under `/individuals/*`, which `App.jsx:617-621`
    // mounts OUTSIDE the auth gate. These writes come from MEMBERS OF THE PUBLIC
    // WITH NO FIREBASE ACCOUNT.
    //
    // "Authentication required everywhere" therefore denies them, and that is
    // what is implemented. Measured consequence, not guessed:
    // `recordTelemetry` catches its own error and returns `false`
    // (`telemetry.js:16-19`), so `await recordTelemetry(...)` NEVER THROWS. The
    // public user still reaches their result page and still sees their plan.
    // Nothing visibly breaks. YOU SIMPLY STOP RECEIVING THE DATA — silently,
    // and the only trace is a console error in a stranger's browser.
    //
    // If that data matters, do NOT just flip this to `if true`. An open create
    // rule on a public collection is an unmetered write endpoint against your
    // billing account with no rate limit rules can impose. The right shape is a
    // Cloud Function (`onCall`, App Check enforced) writing via the Admin SDK —
    // the `publicTriageChat` callable already proves that pattern works for
    // this exact pathway.
    //
    // If you accept the risk knowingly, the interim form is below, commented
    // out. It at least pins the shape and caps the size. Deploying it is a
    // decision with a bill attached, so it does not deploy by default.
    //
    //   match /community_assessments/{assessmentId} {
    //     allow read: if false;
    //     allow create: if request.resource.data.keys().hasAll(['createdAt', 'postalSector'])
    //                   && request.resource.data.createdAt == request.time
    //                   && request.resource.data.postalSector is string
    //                   && request.resource.data.postalSector.size() <= 4
    //                   && request.resource.data.keys().size() <= 20;
    //     allow update, delete: if false;
    //   }
    match /community_assessments/{assessmentId} {
      allow read: if false;
      allow create: if isMember();      // ⚠️ denies the public pathway. By design.
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }


    // ==========================================================================
    // SECTION 11 — PATHS THE CLIENT MUST NOT TOUCH AT ALL
    // ==========================================================================
    //
    // Not listed as separate blocks, because the absence of a rule IS the deny.
    // Recorded here so a future reader knows the omission was deliberate:
    //
    //   `resources/{resourceId}`
    //       The national resource registry. Seeded by
    //       `scripts/firestore_seed.cjs` (Admin SDK, offline) and read ONLY by
    //       `publicTriageChat` (`functions/index.js:688-719`, Admin SDK). No
    //       client code touches it. Both keep working with zero client
    //       permission, so it gets zero.
    //
    //   `demo_*` — e.g. `demo_staff_loads`, `demo_monthly_workload`
    //       `AuraPulseBot.jsx:829` prefixes AURA's DATA_ENTRY target with
    //       `demo_` in sandbox mode, so the simulator writes real documents to a
    //       parallel collection. ⚠️ BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: those writes now fail, and
    //       the DATA_ENTRY error bubble will say so. A sandbox that writes to
    //       the production database is a defect, and the sandbox's own contract
    //       agrees — `RosterView.jsx:457` states "NO FIRESTORE, EVER" for the
    //       roster path. This extends that contract to AURA. Deliberate.
    //
    //   Anything else the language model invents
    //       `AuraPulseBot.jsx:829-835` builds the collection name from
    //       `workload.target_collection`, WHICH COMES OUT OF GEMINI
    //       (`functions/index.js:261`). The prompt asks for `monthly_workload`
    //       or `staff_loads`, but a model is not a validator. Because this file
    //       enumerates paths explicitly and Firestore denies by default, an
    //       invented collection name writes NOTHING. That is arguably the single
    //       largest security win in this proposal, and it comes free from
    //       default-deny.
    //
    // The block below is DOCUMENTATION, not enforcement. Firestore already
    // denies everything unmatched; a rule that evaluates to `false` cannot
    // override an `allow` elsewhere. It is here so that "is there a catch-all?"
    // has a visible answer.
    match /{unmatched=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
